# v2.0.35 (2026-11-10)
# - NEW built-in table %BUILTIN_RESPELL_ANYCASE: 2,655 respellings of the
#     COSCACH spelling review, matched in lowercase after the exact-case table
#     and %BUILTIN_IPA. Letter names are joined with the stress on the last
#     letter (psu peeseú). AND, UP, WoW and RR keep their exact-case readings
#     and lowercase rr stays the backchannel; the exact-case entries that a
#     later decision replaced are gone (K-POP, DC, MMA, YMCA, RPG, TJ, HHI,
#     Yu-Gi-Oh, ACENAF, cds, LAURY, SPEC, ASS, DNA, SELJ, RyB). Three are
#     %BUILTIN_IPA entries: aylwin ˈejl.win (transcribed by hand),
#     revenge re.ˈbenʝ and selj selʝ (their respellings rebény and sely end
#     in a y meant as ʝ, which the y rule below would read as i). Five phrases
#     join @BUILTIN_PHRASES: baby shower, bass house, pokémon go, ray
#     bradbury, scrum master.
# - A built-in entry that ends in a period (J., Dr., C.C.U.) is looked up with
#     its period, before anything else. The period used to be split off first,
#     so such entries never matched: C.C.U. was "se punto se punto u" and Dr.
#     the backchannel dɾ̩.
# - A comma after a word or a number is the short pause | in running text, as
#     it already was with --tokens. normalize_structured_tokens() deleted it
#     before the pause step saw it, so -cmp did nothing there and the narrow
#     rules ran across the comma (la lluvia, bajo gave ˈʋa.xo). -nocmp turns
#     it off; -mfa, -phonword and vertical output keep it off.
# - A written uno is never cut to un. Before any word but a numeral and thirty
#     others (de, que, más, también ...) it became un: uno nunca sabe was
#     un ˈnun.ka ˈsa.be, con uno solo kon un ˈso.lo. A number read before a
#     unit or a currency is still un (1 km, $1).
# - A falling diphthong whose strong vowel carries the written accent (áu, ái,
#     éi, óu, ói) is a diphthong. The glide was left a vowel, so the syllable
#     break went missing: náufrago was ˈnawfɾa.ɡo, máicro ˈmajkɾo, sáurio
#     ˈsawɾjo.
# - i before u and another vowel stays a vowel (aliuén a.li.ˈwen, was
#     a.lj.ˈwen).
# - The -uir hiatus (u.ˈiɾ, kons.tɾu.ˈiɾ, in.klu.ˈi.do) is marked only on the
#     -uir verb forms that have it: the infinitive with its clitics, -uimos,
#     -uiste, -uistes, -uisteis, the participle and -uid, and the forms often
#     written without their accent on i (voseo -uis and -uih, imperfect -uia
#     and the rest). It was marked on every hui and every b, l, t, n or tr
#     before ui, so buitre was bu.ˈi.tɾe, Luis lu.ˈis, huincha u.ˈin.ʧa,
#     intuición in.tu.ˈi.ˈsjon and construiré kons.tɾu.ˈi.ˈɾe (two stresses).
# - The letter y is the vowel i when no vowel is next to it (Lynn, sexy,
#     whisky, nylon, Yvonne; they were lʝ.nn, seksʝ, wis.kʝ, n.ʝlon,
#     ʝ.ˈbon.ne) and the glide between a vowel and a consonant (Reynaldo
#     rej.ˈnal.do, boycott boj.ˈkot; they had ʝ). Before a vowel it is still
#     ʝ, and a final y after a vowel still the glide (rey, Uruguay).
# - A word-final double n is one n (Lynn, Ann, Aislinn; they were linn, ann,
#     ˈajs.linn).
# - Syllables: a vowel, w and a liquid break after w only when the liquid
#     begins a syllable, and a glide after the liquid stays with it (bourbon
#     ˈbowɾ.bon, dinosaurio di.no.ˈsaw.ɾjo; they were ˈbow.ɾ.bon and
#     di.no.ˈsaw.ɾ.jo); a glide j before an obstruent and a liquid closes its
#     syllable, as w did (máicro ˈmaj.kɾo); a u left a vowel by the hiatus
#     marks after a, e or o and before a consonant begins its own syllable
#     (estadounidense es.ta.do.u.ni.ˈden.se, was es.ta.dou.ni.ˈden.se); and no
#     syllable is ever left without a vowel (berns ˈbeɾns, was beɾ.ns).
#     Syllable rule 7 (the prefix sub-) checked for the start of the word
#     only after it had taken the sequence s u b, so inside a word that
#     sequence got no rule at all: microsubrutina was mi.kɾosu.bɾu.ˈti.na,
#     azubuike asu.ˈbwi.ke. The check is now part of the condition.
# - huy- is marked (u a vowel of its own) only before y and a vowel, as in
#     rehuye re.ˈu.ʝe; huy and Chuy end in the glide (uj, ʧuj; they were uʝ
#     and kuʝ).
# - A colon is the long pause everywhere, as a semicolon is: ‖ (|| with -lp2,
#     "(...)" with -nopi). With --tokens, and after a number in running text
#     (a las 12: vamos), it was the short pause | ("(..)" with -nopi). -vrt,
#     the one mode with colon pauses off while semicolon pauses were on, has
#     them on.
# - A full stop after a number is the long pause in running text, as after a
#     word (eran 12. Bajamos). It was deleted, so the narrow rules ran across
#     the sentence break: narrow bajamos was ʋa.ˈxã.mõs (now ba.ˈxã.mõs).
# - --tokens honours -lp2: a full stop, semicolon or colon token was ‖ even
#     with -lp2.
# - Every output file is Unicode NFC, except -mfa dictionaries. The narrow
#     rules build a nasal vowel as the vowel plus U+0303 (the combining
#     tilde); NFC composes it into one character (ã ẽ ĩ õ ũ). Of the 768,019
#     forms of the es-CL dictionary, 490,677 had a narrow transcription that
#     was not NFC, every one because of a nasal vowel; diacritics with no
#     precomposed form (d̪, ɣ̞, n̪) are already NFC and do not change. -mfa
#     output keeps the acoustic model's spelling, which has the nasal vowels
#     decomposed: to MFA a precomposed ã is a phone it has never seen.
# - A number before a noun agrees with it in gender: 1 perro un perro, 1 casa
#     una casa, 21 años veintiún años, 21 horas veintiuna horas, 200 personas
#     doscientas personas, 200 mil personas doscientas mil personas, 21 mil
#     veintiún mil (it was veintiuno mil), in running text and with --tokens.
#     The gender comes from noun_gender(), the common nouns of the es-CL
#     FreeLing dictionary with a single gender (116,000 forms); a word it does
#     not know leaves the number as it was (1 de cada 3 uno de cada tres).
# - A number written with a point and one, two, or four or more digits after
#     it (with the default decimal comma the point only separates thousands)
#     is read as a decimal with punto, and one or two digits after the point
#     as a number: 12.30 doce punto treinta, 3.75 tres punto setenta y cinco,
#     3.05 tres punto cero cinco. Four or more digits are
#     read one by one. The digits after the point were left as digits: doce
#     punto 30.
# - Ordinals: ª is feminine (1.ª primera, 21.ª vigésima primera), and an
#     ordinal written with letters (1a, 2do, 3er, 4to, 21as) is an ordinal
#     whose suffix gives gender and number (1a primera, 2os segundos, 3as
#     terceras; 1er primer, 3er tercer; from 11 on the cardinal, 21a
#     veintiuna). Identifier detection read them as a number and letters
#     (1a uno a, 2do dos de o, 1er un e erre), and ª gave primero.
# - -nopi (non-IPA pause symbols): a full stop, question or exclamation mark
#     after a word is ## and a colon, semicolon, ellipsis or dash (...), as
#     after a number; they printed ‖. A comma is (.) and the stand-ins (.)
#     and (...); they printed | and ‖. With --narrow the rules treat the
#     non-IPA symbols as pauses (a b after (...) was lenited).
# - -noclp and -noscp (and add_colon_pauses or add_semicolon_pauses = 0 in
#     perkins.ini) also delete a colon or semicolon right after a word in
#     running text; it was always the long pause there.
# - Comments: the version tags added in 2.0.32 to 2.0.34 are gone from the
#     code; the changes are recorded here.
#
# v2.0.34 (2026-10-30)
# - --tokens: a token that is only an ellipsis (..., or …) is the long pause,
#     as in running text. It produced nothing, so the narrow rules ran across
#     it (titular ... voy gave voj, where running text gives ‖ boj). Found on
#     the first real COSCACH CoNLL file: 175 ellipses in one interview.
#
# v2.0.33 (2026-10-30)
# - NEW built-in phrase readings, @BUILTIN_PHRASES, matched without regard to
#     case across spaces, underscores and apostrophes: Guns N' Roses (and
#     guns and roses) is read gansenróses; rock and roll, rock n' roll and
#     rock 'n' roll are read rocanról. In running text the phrase is replaced
#     before transcription. With --tokens, when the phrase spans several
#     tokens (rock / and / roll), the reading goes on its first token and the
#     others get an empty field; a joined token (Guns_N_'_Roses) gets it
#     whole.
# - AC/DC (also ACDC, AC-DC) is read eisidísi, ej.si.ˈd̪i.si.
#
# v2.0.32 (2026-10-30)
# Built for the COSCACH phonological layers.
# - NARROW: b, d and ɡ lenite everywhere except after a pause or at the start,
#     after a nasal, and (d only) after l. Coda: v, ð̞, ɣ. Onset after a vowel
#     or glide: ʋ, ð̞, ɣ̞. Onset after another consonant: v, ð̞, ɣ. Until 2.0.31
#     only an onset between two vowels lenited, so lluvia, agua, adiós,
#     deuda, árbol, algo, desde, padre, siglo and iglesia kept their stops.
# - NARROW: velar palatalization also before the glide j (quien cjẽn).
# - NARROW: the -oa stress apostrophe no longer blocks the rules (trabajo
#     with -nt -oa was tɾa.'ba.xo).
# - -phonword now applies the whole Stage-2 tail through finalize_phon_string():
#     output format (-f=CV and the rest), letter case and syllable dots
#     (-nosd, -ss). All three were silently ignored with -pw.
# - -phonword with -wl: exactly one output line per input line, "input line
#     verbatim<TAB>transcription", sub-words joined by a space, empty
#     transcription rather than no line.
# - NEW --tokens / -tok: each input line is a sentence with TAB-separated
#     tokens; each output line has one TAB-separated transcription per token,
#     with the narrow rules applied across the whole sentence.
# - NEW built-in pronunciation tables, %BUILTIN_RESPELL and %BUILTIN_IPA:
#     acronyms and codes read as letters, respelling overrides, backchannels
#     and fillers, from the COSCACH inventory. mm is deliberately absent.
# - XXXREDACTEDXXX and XXXNUMXXX are a long pause (‖, or || with -lp2);
#     XXXUNINTELLIGIBLEXXX is a short pause (|), in every output mode.
# - All-capitals tokens that can be pronounced as a Spanish word are read as
#     words (INACAP, SENAME); only unpronounceable ones are spelled (PDI).
# - Lowercase tokens made only of i v x l c d m are spelled out only when they
#     have no vowel (cd, dvd); mi, di, vivi, clic and other words are read as
#     words, runs of m are left as fillers, and xx is veinte. Until 2.0.31 "mi"
#     was transcribed "eme i".
# - The underscore is a word separator, no longer read as "guión bajo"
#     (still read inside URLs and e-mail addresses).
# - Three or more identical consonant letters collapse to two (rr ll cc nn)
#     or one (garrra garra, zapatilllas zapatillas); runs of m are left alone.
# - The sh and ch backchannel transcriptions lost a stray space before the
#     syllabic mark.
#
# v2.0.31
# - HELP TEXT: --mfa-rules and --mfa-phoneset are now documented in -h, in
#     both languages, under a new "MFA DICTIONARY OPTIONS" / "OPCIONES DEL
#     DICCIONARIO MFA" section placed immediately after the
#     metaconfigurations, since both modify -mfa's output. Neither had ever
#     appeared in -h: the only description of either was its changelog entry
#     here, which meant the options were effectively undiscoverable by anyone
#     who had not read the source.
# - The --mfa-rules entry carries the v2.0.25 scope note (not for the Chilean
#     training path) and the v2.0.19 warning that it must be turned on for a
#     full regeneration rather than piecemeal, since those are the two ways
#     it gets used wrongly.
# - The --mfa-phoneset entry states that the built-in default is NO LONGER
#     the Latin American inventory (v2.0.24), which is the assumption most
#     likely to be carried over from earlier versions.
# - No behaviour change. Help text only.
#
# STILL UNDOCUMENTED IN -h, deliberately left for a decision rather than
# added unasked: --mfa-wl and --mfa-rt. Both are live options renamed in
# v2.0.17 and neither appears in the help either.
#
# NOTE: This changelog has no entries for v2.0.26 through v2.0.30, though
# $version reached 2.0.30. Whatever changed in those five builds is
# recorded nowhere in this file.
#
# v2.0.25
# - /d/ and /g/ now lenite to the APPROXIMANT eth and gamma -- the lowered
#     forms, base plus U+031E -- rather than the plain fricatives, matching
#     the treatment /b/ received in v2.0.24. All three Spanish voiced
#     obstruents are approximants in their lenited Chilean realisations, and
#     writing them as fricatives asserted a stronger constriction than
#     speakers produce.
# - Both lowered forms added to the built-in inventory. The plain fricatives
#     remain legal phones, so a dictionary using either still validates.
#
# - SCOPE NOTE. --mfa-rules is NOT in the training path. The training
#     dictionary is plain -mfa -- canonical phonemes, one pronunciation per
#     word -- and every allophone comes from the project's own allophone
#     table with its context and rank. --mfa-rules exists for work targeting
#     a pretrained model, and should be considered frozen for Chilean
#     purposes. Two places holding allophonic knowledge is one too many.
#
# v2.0.24
# - /b/ now lenites to a LABIODENTAL, not a bilabial: the labiodental
#     approximant between vowels, the labiodental fricative elsewhere.
#     Chilean /b/ is almost always labiodental. In the project's own allophone
#     table both bilabial outcomes -- the fricative beta and the bilabial
#     approximant -- are ranked 0, meaning they essentially never occur.
#     Emitting beta told the aligner to expect a sound Chilean speakers do not
#     produce. Same class of error as the three rules removed in v2.0.22:
#     correct for the Latin American dictionary, wrong here.
# - Consequence: the labiodental nasal is now triggered by ANY labiodental,
#     not by f alone. A nasal before the labiodental fricative or approximant
#     assimilates exactly as it does before f.
# - The labiodental approximant added to the built-in inventory. NOTE the
#     built-in list is therefore NO LONGER the Latin American 43 -- it is a
#     working default for Chilean output. Use --mfa-phoneset with the
#     project's own list once it is settled.
# - /d/ and /g/ are UNCHANGED, still lenite to eth and gamma. Their Chilean
#     allophone rows have not been supplied; do not assume they follow /b/
#     just because /b/ moved.
#
# v2.0.23
# - The voiced palatal affricate is now d + ezh (POSTALVEOLAR), not
#     barred-dotless-j + j-with-crossed-tail. The latter is the MFA Spanish
#     dictionary's convention; this project trains its own acoustic model and
#     does not use it. The two describe different articulations, so this is a
#     phonetic decision, not a notational one.
# - NEW: --mfa-phoneset FILE. Overrides the built-in phone inventory -- one
#     phone per line, blank lines and # comments ignored. The inventory used
#     to be compiled in, which was right while the target was a pretrained
#     model and is wrong now that the project defines its own. Compiling an
#     inventory into a binary makes changing it a rebuild.
# - Aliases extended so nothing silently diverges: the MFA Spanish spelling of
#     the affricate maps onto ours; tied forms map onto untied; and the
#     single-character U+02A4 and U+02A7 map onto their two-character
#     equivalents. All are the SAME phone written differently, and to MFA a
#     one-codepoint difference is a different phone.
#
# v2.0.22
# - THE GOVERNING PRINCIPLE, corrected. v2.0.21 treated agreement with the
#     MFA Spanish dictionary as the target. It is not. That dictionary
#     describes a general Latin American variety; this project transcribes
#     CHILEAN Spanish. MFA aligns audio against whatever phone sequence it is
#     given, so a transcription that says one thing while the speaker produced
#     another aligns worse, not better -- however well it matches a reference
#     lexicon.
#     The dictionary is authoritative for TWO things and no more: the phone
#     INVENTORY, and the exact CHARACTERS used to write each phone. Which
#     phone belongs where is Chilean phonology, and it wins.
#     The comparison harness is therefore a DISCOVERY tool, not a score to
#     maximise. A rising match rate can mean the transcription is drifting
#     away from Chilean.
#
# - THREE RULES REMOVED, all confirmed as not occurring in Chilean Spanish:
#     * Lateral palatalisation before a high front vocoid (672 occurrences in
#       a 10,000-word comparison). Does not happen in Chilean. Added in
#       v2.0.21 purely because the dictionary does it.
#     * Coda obstruent lenition, k to gamma (411) and p to beta (88). Does not
#       happen in Chilean: coda obstruents are weakened or elided, not voiced
#       to a continuant -- and that weakening is variable, so it belongs in
#       rules.yaml rather than baked in here.
#     * s voicing before a voiced consonant (161). Virtually never in Chilean:
#       syllable-final s is ASPIRATED to h or ELIDED. Both are variable and
#       sociolinguistically stratified, so both belong in rules.yaml, where
#       MFA supplies the variants and the acoustics choose. The model already
#       carries an h phone for exactly this.
#
# - KEPT from v2.0.21: the three OVER-APPLICATION fixes, which are corrections
#     to rules that were firing too widely rather than rules copied from the
#     dictionary. n palatalises before high front vocoids only; a nasal
#     followed by a glide is an onset so the preceding vowel stays oral;
#     palatalisation reaches across a rhotic but never a lateral.
#
# - STILL OPEN, deliberately not guessed at: the glide/vowel alternation in
#     hiatus (73 + 58, both directions). Reported as something Perkins should
#     already handle in these contexts -- needs confirming against Perkins'
#     own syllabification before anything is changed.
#
# v2.0.21
# - Six corrections to --mfa-rules, every one derived from a 2000-headword
#     comparison against the real installed dictionary using
#     compare_perkins_to_mfa_dict.py. Baseline before these: 78.7% exact.
#     Counts below are occurrences in that run.
#   NEW -- lateral palatalisation before a high front vocoid (134, the
#     largest single pattern): obligando, eligio, violinista.
#   NEW -- coda obstruent lenition (94): a voiceless stop closing a syllable
#     becomes the matching voiced continuant. k to gamma (77: afecto,
#     autoctona), p to beta (17: adoptiva, tops, capsula).
#   NEW -- s voices to z before a voiced consonant (32): bilinguismo,
#     limosna, esbeltisima.
#   FIX -- n palatalises before HIGH front vocoids only, not all front
#     vocoids (67 over-applications). The dictionary keeps plain n before e:
#     erroneo, contemporaneo, colchones. v2.0.19 was too permissive.
#   FIX -- a nasal followed by a GLIDE is an onset, not a coda, so the
#     preceding vowel stays oral (43 over-applications across all five
#     vowels): in nacimiento the i before m stays oral.
#   FIX -- palatalisation reaches across a RHOTIC only, never a lateral (3):
#     the dictionary keeps k before l in bicicleta, clerigo, nucleo.
# - NOT addressed, visible in the same run and left alone deliberately: the
#     glide/vowel alternation in hiatus (31, both directions) is a
#     SYLLABIFICATION difference rather than allophony; geminate
#     simplification (9); loanword handling -- epenthetic e before initial
#     s-clusters, and English sh (9).
#
# v2.0.20
# - -mfa output may now contain NOTHING outside the acoustic model's phone
#     inventory. Format validity was not enough: an out-of-inventory phone
#     parses perfectly and then fails to align, silently.
#   The built-in inventory is the REAL one -- 43 phones, read from an
#     installed dictionary by codepoint dump. NOT the 33 on the published
#     documentation page, which omits ten of them including every nasalised
#     vowel.
#   ENCODING ALIASES are rewritten to the dictionary's own spelling, byte for
#     byte: precomposed nasalised vowels, decomposed c-cedilla, tied
#     affricates, ASCII g. Same phone, different bytes -- and to MFA a
#     one-codepoint difference is a different phone.
#   GENUINELY DIFFERENT PHONES are REJECTED, not mapped. Mapping the dental
#     nasal or a lowered fricative onto something else would silently convert
#     one phone into another and hide that --narrow is the wrong mode here.
#     The entry is dropped and reported with codepoints.
#
# v2.0.19
# - NEW: --mfa-rules. Applies the Montreal Forced Aligner Spanish
#     dictionary's OWN allophonic rules to -mfa output, using its OWN exact
#     characters. --narrow is untouched and keeps serving its own purposes;
#     this is a separate routine for a separate job.
# - WHY a separate routine rather than reusing --narrow: the target is not
#     "correct Chilean allophony", it is "whatever the acoustic model was
#     trained to expect". The model's beta means whatever the dictionary
#     called beta. Emitting a phonetically defensible symbol in a different
#     environment than the dictionary used produces a phone the model has
#     never seen in that context -- and no phone-set audit can detect it,
#     because the phone IS in the inventory. Wrong placement is silent.
# - EVERY rule and EVERY codepoint below was derived by measurement from the
#     installed spanish_latin_america_mfa.dict (84,570 entries) using
#     extract_mfa_allophone_rules.py. None of it is inferred from Spanish
#     phonology, and where measurement and theory disagreed, measurement won.
#
#     RULE 1 -- velar palatalisation of k and x. Before a front vocoid,
#     transparently across at most one liquid: k -> c, x -> c-cedilla.
#     The liquid transparency is measured, not assumed: c is followed by a
#     rhotic in 38.5% of its occurrences (anglicana = ...j-with-stroke +
#     turned-y + i...), which no "before front vowels" rule predicts.
#
#     RULE 2 -- spirantisation. Stop after a pause or a nasal; for the dental
#     d only, also after a lateral. Continuant everywhere else. Measured:
#     b takes a stop context 99.5% of the time (word edge 75.7% + m 23.8%),
#     dental d 99.8% (edge 57.4% + n 39.4% + lateral 3.0%), g 99.8% (edge
#     72.6% + eng 27.2%). The lateral asymmetry is real: after a lateral,
#     b spirantises (3.3% of betas) while dental d does not.
#
#     RULE 3 -- palatalisation of the remaining g stop, to dotless-j-with-
#     stroke, in the same environment as rule 1. It runs AFTER spirantisation
#     because the dictionary shows this phone in the stop environment (word
#     edge 58.2%, palatal nasal 37.0%) -- it is the palatalised STOP, and
#     gamma has no palatalised counterpart in the inventory at all.
#
#     RULE 4 -- nasal place assimilation. n takes the place of the following
#     consonant: m before labials, m-with-hook before f (measured at 99.5%),
#     n-with-left-hook before palatals, eng before velars (k/g/x at 99.4%).
#     It runs AFTER palatalisation because a palatalised velar takes the
#     palatal nasal, not eng -- measured directly in the dictionary.
#
#     RULE 5 -- vowel nasalisation, before a CODA nasal only. This is the
#     rule most easily got wrong: a vowel before an ONSET nasal does NOT
#     nasalise. Measured directly -- abanico has a plain a before the palatal
#     nasal, abanderada has a nasalised a before a coda n. The trigger is a
#     nasal followed by a consonant or a word boundary, not a nasal as such.
#
# - EXACT CHARACTERS, all confirmed by codepoint dump of the real dictionary:
#     beta is GREEK small letter beta U+03B2, not any Latin character;
#     nasalised vowels are DECOMPOSED, base vowel + U+0303, never precomposed;
#     the affricates are UNTIED (t + esh, and dotless-j-with-stroke + j-with-
#     crossed-tail), no tie bar; c is plain ASCII c; c-cedilla is precomposed
#     U+00E7; script g is U+0261, which Perkins has emitted since v2.0.4.
# - Default is OFF. The current master pronunciation dictionary is valid
#     against the model as it stands, and switching this on silently would
#     invalidate every comparison against it. Turn it on deliberately, and
#     turn it on for a full regeneration rather than piecemeal.
#
# v2.0.18
# - PHONE-INVENTORY GUARD for -mfa output. No phone token in the
#     pronunciation column may carry a stress mark (IPA primary U+02C8 or
#     secondary U+02CC) or an acute accent (precomposed a-acute etc., or a
#     combining U+0301). Any that appear are stripped and counted, and the
#     count is reported at end of run.
# - WHY this is a correctness issue and not cosmetics: MFA/Kaldi treat every
#     space-delimited token in the pronunciation column as an ATOMIC PHONE
#     SYMBOL, compared as a string. A stressed vowel therefore is not "the
#     same vowel, marked" -- it is a DIFFERENT PHONE. Three consequences,
#     all bad: (1) the vowel inventory silently doubles, splitting each
#     vowel's acoustic training data between two symbols that model the
#     same sound; (2) against a PRETRAINED acoustic model, the stressed
#     variants are simply not in its inventory at all, so those entries
#     cannot align; (3) triphone context questions are computed over a
#     phone set that no longer matches the phonology. This is the same
#     reason ARPAbet-based lexicons collapse AA0/AA1/AA2 to AA.
# - This was ALREADY the actual behaviour, because -mfa's metaconfig forces
#     $no_stress_marks=1 (v2.0.9), which in turn forces
#     $stress_using_tildes=0. Confirmed by direct audit: over a deliberately
#     accent-heavy word list, -mfa produces a 23-phone inventory and -mfa
#     --narrow a 31-phone inventory, neither containing a single stress mark
#     or accented vowel. Confirmed too that neither -st nor a perkins.ini
#     setting can force stress back in, since the metaconfig runs last.
#     The guard exists because that is a CONSEQUENCE OF CONFIGURATION, not a
#     guarantee: one edit to the metaconfig block, or one new mode
#     interaction, and the output regresses silently with nothing to catch
#     it. The invariant is now stated where the output is written.
# - The ORTHOGRAPHIC column is deliberately untouched. Accents there are
#     required, not tolerated: MFA looks a word up by matching the
#     transcript's own orthography, so "medico" without its accent would
#     simply fail to match "medico" with one. Only the phone column is
#     policed.
#
# v2.0.17
# - RENAMED -g2p TO -mfa THROUGHOUT: the flag itself, --g2p-wl/--g2p-rt
#     (now --mfa-wl/--mfa-rt) and their long forms, the perkins.ini key
#     (g2p_format -> mfa_format), every internal variable and subroutine
#     name, the help text in both languages, the comments, and the default
#     output extension (.G2P -> .MFA). The mode has only ever existed to
#     feed the Montreal Forced Aligner; the name now says so.
# - Passing -g2p, --g2p-wl or --g2p-rt is now a HARD ERROR (exit 2) naming
#     the replacement, rather than a silent alias. An alias would let every
#     existing caller keep working while the project's own scripts drifted
#     apart from the tool again, which is the failure mode this project has
#     already hit more than once.
# - MFA OUTPUT-FORMAT ENFORCEMENT: every dictionary line is now written
#     through emit_mfa_entry() rather than printed directly, so the output
#     satisfies MFA's documented format instead of merely resembling it.
#     Verified against MFA 3.x's own specification: exactly one TAB between
#     word and pronunciation (a hard requirement since MFA 2.0.5, not a
#     convention); phones separated by non-tab whitespace (a pronunciation
#     with no internal spaces is read as ONE phone -- silently, which makes
#     it the worst failure mode available); no tab inside the pronunciation;
#     no whitespace inside the headword; no collision with MFA's reserved
#     sil/spn/<unk>/<eps> tokens. Entries that cannot be made valid are
#     DROPPED and counted, never repaired into something plausible.
# - Exact duplicate word+pronunciation lines are suppressed. A repeated
#     headword with a DIFFERENT pronunciation is left alone: that is how MFA
#     expresses pronunciation variants, and MFA 3.0 estimates per-variant
#     probabilities during training.
# - A word with no phonemes at all is dropped from .MFA output. Perkins
#     still emits such a headword in other modes (v2.0.2, so a zero-phoneme
#     word could not silently shift every later line by one), but a headword
#     with an empty pronunciation column is not a valid MFA entry. The
#     v2.0.2 failure it prevented cannot recur here, since the drop is
#     counted and the first ten are named.
# - Corrected the -mfa help examples, in both languages, which were stale by
#     four versions: they showed stress marks (removed in v2.0.9), ASCII g
#     instead of IPA g (fixed in v2.0.4), and no dental diacritic (added in
#     v2.0.8). Replaced with output generated by actually running this
#     version. The -phonword examples were left alone -- they show stress
#     because that mode legitimately produces it.
#
# (There is no v2.0.16. That number was consumed by a mis-numbered build
#  and is skipped rather than reused, so no two files can claim it.)
#
# v2.0.15
# - Fixed a real, PRE-EXISTING bug found by adversarial --coscach/-vrt/
#     -mfa testing requested directly: do_vrt_preprocessing() blanketly
#     converted every space to ¬ (intended to protect Connexor-style
#     multi-word units like "estados unidos"), which also swallowed the
#     spaces immediately around ANY bracketed sentinel token -- meaning
#     @word_array = split(/ /, ...) later had no space left to split on
#     around it, so the entire line became one single "word" instead of
#     separate tokens, and the sentinel never reached the ERROR SENTINEL
#     BYPASS at all. Confirmed this predates today's work entirely: the
#     already-existing [InvalidDate] sentinel was equally affected in
#     -vrt mode, not just the new [UtteranceBoundary] one -- "el numero
#     12/34/56/78/90/11 es raro" via -vrt produced garbled, phonemicized
#     nonsense from the bracketed token instead of leaving it untouched.
#     Fixed with a targeted regex change (excluding only spaces directly
#     touching a bracket from the ¬ conversion), verified to leave the
#     original Connexor multi-word-unit behavior completely unaffected.
# - Verified --coscach mode works correctly with --narrow across every
#     rule category (spirantization, the after-l exception, velar
#     palatalization with tilde-marked stressed vowels, nasal place
#     assimilation including the palatal case, the ‖->|| pause-symbol
#     conversion correctly still blocking cross-word rules) -- no bugs
#     found there.
# - Verified -mfa (both submodes) and -phonword handle utterance
#     boundaries correctly at additional edge cases beyond what v2.0.14
#     covered: a boundary at the very start or very end of a -mfa
#     one-word-per-line headword, and multiple boundaries within a
#     single headword (each clause's own rules fire independently, with
#     no cross-clause pollution and no stray whitespace artifacts from
#     the multiple strip operations).
# - Found, but explicitly NOT fixed today, a separate, deeper, genuinely
#     pre-existing -vrt architectural quirk, confirmed unrelated to
#     --narrow (identical behavior in the untouched v2.0.8 baseline):
#     when a single input line produces multiple output lines in -vrt
#     mode ($vertical_output=1), Stage 2's read/chomp/rewrite cycle
#     never re-inserts a newline or space at the point each line was
#     chomped, so those lines end up silently concatenated with no
#     separator in the final output. This is purely cosmetic/structural
#     -- confirmed directly that --narrow's actual phonological rules
#     still fire and block correctly regardless (a boundary still
#     correctly prevents a cross-word rule from firing even though the
#     visual space around the resulting ‖ symbol is missing) -- but
#     worth knowing about and flagged here rather than silently
#     patched over, since properly fixing -vrt's own newline handling
#     is a separate, larger undertaking than today's --narrow-focused
#     testing pass.
# - Re-ran the full 41-combination regression suite after all of the
#     above: all 41 still pass. Also re-verified at full ~1,245-word
#     corpus scale specifically for -vrt + --narrow: no crashes, valid
#     UTF-8, zero sentinel leaks, 45 correctly-placed pause markers.
#
# v2.0.14
# - Fixed a real latent bug found by further, deliberately adversarial
#     testing after v2.0.13 shipped: -mfa's one-word-per-line mode
#     (which treats an entire input line as one headword) SKIPPED the
#     new [UtteranceBoundary] sentinel entirely rather than converting
#     it to something that would actually block cross-word rules --
#     meaning the boundary information was silently deleted right
#     before narrow_transcription() ran on the joined multi-word
#     string. Confirmed directly: "un pan; beso" via -mfa --mfa-wl
#     --narrow wrongly produced "unpanbeso -> ũ m p ã ɱ v e s o"
#     (nb->ɱv firing straight across the semicolon), while default mode
#     on the same input correctly produced "ũm.pãn ‖ ˈbe.so".
# - Fixed by pushing the real ‖ character in place of the sentinel
#     (instead of skipping it) before narrow_transcription() runs, so
#     it correctly blocks cross-word rules exactly as it already does
#     everywhere else -- then stripping ‖ back out of the finished
#     dictionary entry afterward, since a literal pause symbol has no
#     place in a MFA headword's phoneme string, narrowed or not.
# - Verified: the exact bug case now correctly produces "unpanbeso ->
#     ũ m p ã n b e s o" (blocked); multiple utterance boundaries
#     within a single headword also verified correct ("un pan; un
#     beso; un guiso" -> each clause's own rules fire independently,
#     with no cross-clause pollution and no stray double-spaces or ‖
#     leaks from the multiple strip operations); full 41-combination
#     regression suite re-run afterward, all passing, with an
#     additional explicit scan confirming zero ‖ or sentinel leaks in
#     -mfa output at full corpus scale.
# - This was found specifically by testing further after being asked
#     directly whether any latent bugs might remain -- worth recording
#     as a reminder that the answer to that kind of question is never
#     an unqualified "no", even right after a clean full regression
#     pass; only "no further bugs found in the areas checked so far".
#
# v2.0.13
# - Fixed the real bug found during large-scale stress-testing on real
#     Spanish Wikipedia prose: --narrow's cross-word rules (e.g. nb ->
#     ɱv) could fire across clause punctuation (periods, semicolons,
#     etc.) that never actually produced a real pause marker, since
#     normalize_structured_tokens() silently discarded that punctuation
#     long before any pause-detection code ever saw it. Root cause
#     traced precisely: the punctuation was stripped at the LINE level
#     (before word-splitting), while the existing sentence_break/pause-
#     insertion logic only ran per-WORD, later -- by the time it ran,
#     the punctuation was already gone. Confirmed directly: "una
#     insurrección; Valdivia" -> --narrow wrongly produced "...sjõɱ.val..."
#     across the semicolon.
# - Fixed by having normalize_structured_tokens() replace utterance-
#     boundary punctuation with a bracketed sentinel, [UtteranceBoundary]
#     (matching the existing [MalformedNumber]-style convention), instead
#     of discarding it. This sentinel survives word-splitting and
#     phonemicization untouched (added to the existing ERROR SENTINEL
#     BYPASS) and is converted to the real ‖ symbol during Stage 2
#     cleanup, right where word_level_syllab_adjust() and narrow_
#     transcription() already run -- both already correctly treat ‖ as
#     a hard boundary, confirmed separately; neither needed any changes.
# - Utterance-boundary characters: ., ... (three literal periods, via
#     the existing "+" quantifier), !, ?, :, and ; -- plus, per direct
#     instruction, em dash (—) and the single-character ellipsis symbol
#     (…, U+2026, distinct from three periods). Comma is deliberately
#     NOT included -- it remains a separate, shorter pause ($comma_pause),
#     stripped exactly as before. Em dash and ellipsis use a simpler,
#     unconditional substitution (not requiring a preceding letter
#     sequence) since neither is reliably attached directly to a word --
#     em dash in particular is commonly a standalone parenthetical
#     marker. Confirmed this runs before fix_misc_punctuation()'s
#     existing em-dash-to-"--" conversion, so the sentinel correctly
#     intercepts the raw — character first.
# - -mfa (both submodes) and -phonword needed their own explicit skip
#     for this new sentinel, since (like narrow_transcription() itself,
#     fixed in v2.0.11) they exit before the Stage-2 pass where it's
#     normally converted. Found as a real leak during testing: without
#     the skip, -mfa tried to phonemicize the sentinel as if it were an
#     ordinary word, producing garbled output ("utteranceboundary" ->
#     nonsense phonemes); -phonword showed the raw bracketed text as its
#     own spurious dictionary entry. Both now correctly skip it entirely
#     -- a pause marker has no meaningful entry in either a MFA
#     dictionary or a phonword tier. Default mode and every other output
#     mode reach Stage 2 normally and needed no changes; verified this
#     scoping is exact, not just "probably fine."
# - Added a hard error for combining --narrow with CV, CVG, CVNLRG, or
#     VOICING output: per direct instruction, these formats reduce
#     every phoneme to a single category letter, and --narrow's
#     allophonic distinctions (e.g. spirantized vs. stop /b/) collapse
#     to the same letter either way, so the combination can never
#     change the output -- confirmed during the same stress-testing
#     session that it was silently producing output that looked
#     plausible but didn't reflect --narrow at all (worse than useless).
#     Checked right after forcibly_set_variables() resolves the final
#     state of every relevant flag, before any file processing begins.
# - Manner and place output, by contrast, ARE made to work correctly
#     with --narrow, per direct instruction, since --narrow's new
#     phonemes DO sometimes land in different manner/place categories
#     (e.g. stop vs. fricative /b/ are different manners). Added every
#     new --narrow phoneme (ʋ, ð̞, ɣ̞, ɣ, ɟ, ç, ʝ̞, ɱ, c) to the
#     appropriate character-class variables ($plosives, $fricatives,
#     $aproximants, $nasals, $labiodentals, $dentals, $palatals,
#     $velars). Found and fixed two real bugs in the process, not just
#     additions:
#     1. n̪ (dental nasal, from --narrow's nasal place assimilation) was
#        being classified as alveolar ("A") in place mode instead of
#        dental ("D") -- $alveolars' own bare "n" entry was catching it
#        before $dentals got a chance to, since $dentals lacked an n̪
#        entry of its own. Fixed by adding n̪ explicitly to $dentals
#        (which already correctly runs before $alveolars in the
#        existing substitution order).
#     2. ɣ̞/ʝ̞ (the "lowered", approximant-quality outputs of intervocalic
#        spirantization/palatalization) share their base character (ɣ,
#        ʝ) with an existing bare entry already in $fricatives -- since
#        $fricatives ran before $aproximants in opt_convert_phonemes_
#        manners()'s existing substitution order, it would have wrongly
#        consumed just the base character as a plain fricative before
#        $aproximants ever saw the full sequence. Fixed by reordering:
#        $aproximants now runs before $fricatives specifically in that
#        function. (Not an issue for place mode: ɣ and ɣ̞ correctly map
#        to the same place category either way, so no reordering was
#        needed there.)
# - Verified manner and place output phoneme-by-phoneme (not just
#     spot-checked) against every rule-coverage word from the velar-
#     palatalization work: "un beso, un guiso, la bota, aguila, seguir,
#     tengo, queso" -- every single phoneme in both modes now lands in
#     a real, correct category letter; zero unclassified fallthrough
#     characters remain anywhere in either mode's output.
# - Re-ran the full 41-combination stress-test suite (every output mode
#     x with/without --narrow, plus 13 additional flag-stacking
#     combinations) after all of the above: all 41 pass, including the
#     4 that now correctly error (CV/CVG/CVNLRG/voicing + --narrow) and
#     the one that fires 33 correct pause markers across the full
#     ~1,245-word real Wikipedia corpus with zero sentinel leaks and
#     zero UTF-8 corruption.
#
# v2.0.12
# - Added a new --narrow rule (rule 9): velar palatalization before a
#     front vowel (/i/ or /e/, stressed or not) -- k -> c, x -> ç,
#     ɡ -> ɟ, ɣ -> ʝ, ɣ̞ -> ʝ̞. Runs AFTER spirantization (rules 4-7), so
#     it correctly sees whichever surface form (stop vs. continuant)
#     those rules already produced for /ɡ/ specifically (ɟ only catches
#     ɡ that's still a stop -- utterance-initial or after a nasal --
#     since an intervocalic or word-final ɡ before a front vowel has
#     already become ɣ̞ or ɣ by the time this rule runs); and BEFORE
#     nasal place assimilation (renumbered rule 10), so a nasal
#     preceding a newly-palatalized consonant correctly assimilates to
#     palatal place, not the velar place the consonant no longer has.
# - Rule 10 (nasal place assimilation)'s palatal-triggering consonant
#     list expanded to include the new palatalized velars (c/ç/ɟ)
#     alongside the original tʃ/ʝ/ɟʝ/ɲ set. Verified directly: "un
#     guiso" -> uɲɟiso (/n/ -> ɲ before the newly-palatalized ɟ, not the
#     ŋ it would otherwise get before a plain velar).
# - ɣ̞ -> ʝ̞ (not a bare "j" glide, as briefly considered and rejected
#     during design): this was deliberately corrected specifically to
#     avoid a real symbol collision -- Perkins already uses plain "j"
#     as its default representation for the ordinary rising-diphthong
#     glide /j/ (e.g. "unión", "hielo"), completely unrelated to
#     anything in this rule. Had rule 9's ɣ̞ output stayed as bare "j",
#     rule 10's expanded palatal list would have also wrongly triggered
#     nasal palatalization before every ordinary glide "j" the language
#     already has, not just the newly-palatalized velar-derived one.
#     Using the distinct ʝ̞ (ʝ + the same lowering diacritic already used
#     in ɣ̞) sidesteps this entirely, and "j" was accordingly REMOVED
#     from rule 10's palatal list (it was only ever there because of
#     the old, since-corrected ɣ̞->j output; keeping it would have
#     reintroduced the exact ordinary-glide collision this fixes).
#     Verified directly: "unión" via --narrow now correctly stays
#     ũ.njõn (n unchanged, only vowel nasalization applies) instead of
#     the wrongly-palatalized ũɲjõn an uncorrected "j" output would
#     have produced.
# - Verified every new sub-rule individually against real Perkins
#     output plus full regression on the entire prior rule set:
#     "queso" -> ce.so (k -> c); "kilo" -> ci.lo (k -> c); "jinete" ->
#     çi.ne.t̪e (x -> ç, with correct incidental vowel nasalization from
#     the following /n/); "un guiso" -> uɲɟi.so (ɡ -> ɟ blocked-context
#     + nasal palatalization); "seguir" -> se.ʝ̞iɾ and "aguila" ->
#     a.ʝ̞i.la (intervocalic ɡ -> ɣ̞ -> ʝ̞); "tengo" -> t̪ẽŋ.ɡo (ɡ correctly
#     NOT palatalized before "o", a non-front vowel); "nan" -> nãn
#     (idempotent double-sided nasalization, unaffected by any of
#     today's changes); "unión" -> ũ.njõn (glide-collision fix,
#     confirmed above).
#
# v2.0.11
# - Real bug, reported directly: "-mfa -narrow" produced completely
#     un-narrowed output -- --narrow silently had no effect at all when
#     combined with -mfa (or -phonword). Root cause: both modes write
#     their "already in final form" output directly during the first
#     pass and then exit BEFORE the Stage-2 cleanup pass, which is where
#     narrow_transcription() was being called. Fixed by calling it
#     directly at each mode's own output-writing point instead.
# - That first fix immediately surfaced a second, real bug of its own:
#     applying narrow_transcription() to each word's phoneme string
#     separately (per iteration of the per-word loop, before joining)
#     meant cross-word rules (nb -> ɱv) could never see the boundary
#     between two words at all, since each word was transformed in
#     total isolation -- confirmed directly ("un beso" produced "ũ n"
#     and "v e s o" as two independently-narrowed fragments, never
#     "ũɱ v e s o"). Fixed for -mfa's one-word-per-line mode by
#     deferring narrow_transcription() until AFTER all of a line's
#     words are joined into one string with real spaces -- exactly
#     mirroring how the Stage-2 pass already receives a whole,
#     multi-word line. -mfa's running-text mode is a genuine exception,
#     not a further bug: each word there becomes its own independent,
#     reusable dictionary entry with no next-word context available at
#     all, so cross-word rules cannot apply there by design -- narrow_
#     transcription() is still applied per-word in that mode, but only
#     the rules that don't need cross-word context can have any effect.
# - A third, deeper problem surfaced while testing the second fix: -mfa
#     mode's OWN output convention (when $space_between_phonemes==1,
#     -mfa's default) puts a literal space between EVERY phoneme, not
#     just between words -- indistinguishable, to narrow_transcription()'s
#     own rules, from a genuine word-boundary space. Confirmed directly:
#     a single isolated word, "beso" (phonemes "b e s o"), had its
#     word-INITIAL "b" wrongly treated as word-FINAL and turned into
#     "v", since rule 3's word-final pattern matches "b" followed by any
#     space, and -mfa's own per-phoneme spacing put one right after it.
#     Fixed with a placeholder (\x01): each word's own internal -mfa
#     phoneme-spacing is converted to \x01 before narrow_transcription()
#     ever sees it (so it's correctly ignored as a within-word boundary,
#     same as a period or stress mark already was), while genuine
#     word-boundary spaces (from joining separate words) are left as
#     real spaces so cross-word rules still fire correctly across them;
#     \x01 is converted back to a plain space afterward to match -mfa's
#     expected output format. narrow_transcription()'s own $SEP
#     definition now includes \x01 alongside the existing separator
#     characters.
# - Verified all three fixes together against every relevant
#     combination: "un beso" via -mfa --mfa-wl --narrow now correctly
#     produces "unbeso\tũ ɱ v e s o" (cross-word rule fires); an
#     isolated "beso" via the same flags correctly stays "b e s o"
#     (no longer wrongly narrowed); -mfa --mfa-rt --narrow correctly
#     narrows each word independently with no cross-word effect
#     (documented limitation, not a bug); -phonword --narrow unaffected
#     by any of this (regression-checked); default mode with --narrow
#     unaffected (regression-checked); -mfa without --narrow unaffected
#     (regression-checked).
#
# v2.0.10
# - Documentation completeness pass for --narrow/-nt (added in v2.0.9,
#     but never documented anywhere -- same category of gap as the
#     v1.9.110/v1.9.111/v1.9.112 documentation audits). Added: a new
#     "NARROW (ALLOPHONIC) TRANSCRIPTION" section to -h help text in
#     both languages; a real, verified usage example in -u (both
#     languages), generated by actually running the standard usage-
#     guide source text through --narrow rather than hand-written, and
#     confirmed to exercise nearly every rule at once (cross-word
#     spirantization in both directions, the blocked-after-consonant
#     case, all three nasal-place-assimilation environments including
#     palatal /ɲ/, and vowel nasalization from every nasal type);
#     narrow_transcription added to the perkins.ini whitelist
#     (_ALLOWED_CFG_VARS), the -d debug dump, and the ACTIVE SETTINGS
#     table shown on every normal run (its own new section, matching
#     STRESS/SYLLABLES/PAUSES etc.). Also added a new, comprehensive
#     README documenting every rule in the ruleset in full detail,
#     separate from this script's own inline comments.
#
# v2.0.9
# - Added --narrow/-nt: a new, independent transcription mode applying
#     Chilean Spanish phonologically-conditioned narrow-transcription
#     rules on top of whatever phonemic output the current mode already
#     produces (works the same way for -mfa, running text, phonword,
#     etc. -- not tied to any one output mode). Implements only the
#     rules confirmed to be fully determined by phonological context --
#     b/d/g spirantization (intervocalic and word-final, including the
#     "blocked after l" exception for d), yeismo affrication (ɟʝ -> dʒ
#     utterance-initially, ʝ elsewhere), full nasal place assimilation
#     for /n/ (m never changes on its own; nb -> ɱv is a special atomic
#     case since it's based on b's own already-spirantized surface
#     place, not the underlying phoneme), and vowel nasalization (direct
#     1-segment V-N/N-V contact only, using the nasal's already-
#     assimilated surface form). Deliberately does NOT implement the
#     free-variation phenomena identified alongside these rules
#     (fricative-vs-approximant spirantization quality, d-elision,
#     /theta/ as a sociolinguistic allophone of /s/) -- those aren't
#     determinable from text alone and are left to the phone recognizer
#     / MFA rules.yaml instead, per direct instruction.
# - New subroutine narrow_transcription(), modeled on the same
#     pattern as the existing word_level_syllab_adjust() (regex
#     substitutions over the whole line's phoneme string, crossing word
#     boundaries but never a pause) -- called independently of
#     $syllabify_by_sentence, right after it in the output cleanup pass.
# - Confirmed necessary during testing: a "boundary" between two
#     phonemes at this pipeline stage can be nothing, a word-boundary
#     space, a syllable dot, or a stress mark (word_level_syllab_adjust
#     and per-word stress-marking have already run by this point) --
#     every rule uses a general separator class tolerating all of these,
#     not a bare optional space, confirmed against real output rather
#     than assumed.
# - Verified rule-by-rule and in combination against real Perkins output
#     (not just a standalone harness): "un beso" -> "ũɱ.ˈve.so", "la
#     bota" -> "la.ˈʋo.t̪a", "el chico anda" -> "el.ˈt͡ʃi.ko.ˈãn̪.d̪a" (n
#     correctly dentalizes before d̪, which itself correctly stays a
#     stop since it isn't intervocalic), and confirmed a real pause
#     marker correctly blocks cross-boundary rules. Found and fixed one
#     real ordering bug in the process: the palatal nasal-assimilation
#     rule (tʃ/ʝ/ɟʝ/ɲ) must be checked before the dental one (t̪/d̪/t/d),
#     since "tʃ"'s leading "t" would otherwise be greedily consumed by
#     the dental rule's bare "t" alternative first.
# - -mfa mode's own metaconfig preset now forces $no_stress_marks=1
#     (was forced to 0 through v2.0.8, meaning stress WAS being marked
#     by default in -mfa output). Confirmed as a real, direct instruction
#     following a downstream finding: stress baked into phone identity
#     (e.g. "ˈa" as a phone distinct from plain "a") needlessly fragments
#     acoustic-model training data across stress variants of what is
#     linguistically the same phone -- the same well-documented reason
#     ARPAbet-based pipelines commonly strip stress digits (AA0/AA1/AA2
#     -> AA) before MFA-style forced alignment. -mfa mode's entire
#     purpose is producing a pronunciation dictionary for exactly this
#     kind of downstream use, so this is now off by default specifically
#     for that mode. Every other output mode (running-text phonemic
#     transcription used for TextGrid tiers, etc.) is unaffected --
#     $no_stress_marks is set independently per mode's own metaconfig
#     block, not as a single global default.
#
# v2.0.8
# - Dental diacritics (d̪/t̪) were never actually reaching -mfa output,
#     even though -mfa's own metaconfig preset unconditionally forces
#     $use_dental_diacr=1 (this has been true since v2.0.3, per that
#     version's "Added dental diacritics to the --mfa output metaconfig"
#     changelog entry -- confirmed the preset itself was never the
#     problem). The diacritics WERE being added upstream, correctly, on
#     every run. They were then being silently stripped right back out
#     by build_mfa_phoneme_string()'s final character whitelist, which
#     simply never included the dental diacritic character (U+032A) --
#     so no CLI flag or variable setting could have made them survive;
#     the whitelist itself was the dead end, unconditionally, regardless
#     of $use_dental_diacr's value.
# - Added U+032A to that whitelist. Not sufficient on its own, though:
#     split // (used to walk the string one phoneme at a time) treats a
#     combining diacritic as its own separate character, so without
#     further changes it would have become a stray token of its own
#     (e.g. "d ̪ e" instead of "d̪ e"). Added a merge rule -- alongside the
#     existing "tʃ" merge rule, same idea -- so U+032A right after a "d"
#     or "t" is consumed as part of that same phoneme, not pushed as its
#     own token.
# - Verified: "de" -> "d̪ e", "tanto" -> "t̪ ˈa n t̪ o", correctly merged
#     (not split into separate diacritic tokens) with $use_dental_diacr
#     at its -mfa-forced default of 1. Re-ran all four prior regression
#     tests (word list, 84-word paragraph, apostrophe test, mcdonalds
#     stress test) -- every "d"/"t" now correctly shows its dental
#     diacritic, and nothing else (word identity, stress placement,
#     apostrophe handling) changed.
#
# v2.0.7
# - "mcdonalds" (no apostrophe) was getting stress on the wrong syllable:
#     "mak.do.náld" instead of "mak.dó.nald". Root cause: an existing rule
#     (documented above as "lds > ld", written for exactly this word)
#     strips the final "s" from any word ending in "...lds". Once that
#     "s" is gone, "mcdonalds" ends in "d" -- a consonant other than n/s
#     -- so the regular Spanish default stress rule (non-n/s-final
#     consonant = stress the LAST syllable) fires and puts stress on
#     "náld". The apostrophe form, "mcdonald's", was never affected by
#     any of this, since the apostrophe blocks the "lds > ld" rule from
#     matching in the first place (it isn't a literal "...lds" substring
#     once there's an apostrophe in the way) -- so it already had the
#     right stress before this fix, on "dó".
# - Added a lexical-exception correction, "mak.do.náld" -> "mak.dó.nald",
#     in the same block (and same style) as the file's existing foreign-
#     loanword stress exceptions (Microsoft, Craigslist, Portland, etc.)
#     right below it -- this is exactly that same category of problem,
#     just not yet covered.
# - Verified: both "mcdonalds" and "mcdonald's" now correctly stress
#     "dó"/"do". Re-ran all three of v2.0.6's regression tests (5-word
#     list, 84-word Casado paragraph, the apostrophe test file) and
#     confirmed byte-for-byte identical output except for this one
#     intended change.
#
# v2.0.6
# - -mfa was silently colliding pairs like "mcdonald's"/"mcdonalds" and
#     "o'higgins"/"ohiggins" onto the same dictionary headword, sometimes
#     with two different, contradictory pronunciations under one key.
#     Traced to the ACTUAL root cause rather than patched where it was
#     first noticed: buffer_word() -- used by EVERY output mode, not just
#     -mfa -- stripped apostrophes from the orthographic form before it
#     ever reached @output_ortho, via a blanket "keep only \p{L}" filter.
# - Added clean_orthographic_word(): keeps letters and INTERNAL
#     apostrophes (straight ' or curly ’), but still strips a LEADING or
#     TRAILING one (almost always a quotation mark around the word, e.g.
#     'hola', not part of the word itself). Does not change case --
#     other output modes besides -mfa want original capitalization kept.
#     buffer_word() now calls this instead of its old blanket filter.
# - build_mfa_dict_key() (added in v2.0.5 for the two -mfa headword-
#     building call sites) is now a thin wrapper: lowercased
#     clean_orthographic_word(). Both -mfa paths already called it, so no
#     further change was needed there once the real fix landed upstream.
# - Verified the fix actually reaches BOTH -mfa paths, not just the one
#     where the bug was first noticed: re-ran a word-list file containing
#     "mcdonald's"/"mcdonalds"/"o'higgins"/"ohiggins"/'hola'/"pa'l" (every
#     one now a distinct, correctly-keyed entry, and the quoted 'hola'
#     correctly becomes "hola", not "'hola'"), AND a running-text sentence
#     containing the same forms in prose (previously still merged them,
#     since it reads @output_ortho -- now produces the same correct
#     distinct entries). Also re-ran both of v2.0.5's original end-to-end
#     regression tests (the 5-word list, and the 84-word Casado
#     paragraph) and confirmed byte-for-byte identical output to before
#     this fix -- nothing else changed.
#
# v2.0.5
# - -mfa previously treated EVERY input line as exactly one dictionary
#     headword, full stop -- correct for a real one-word-per-line word
#     list, but meaningless for running text (a sentence's worth of
#     words glued into one giant "headword" with no spaces, since
#     non-letter characters -- including spaces -- are stripped when
#     building the headword). Verified this against the actual
#     unmodified v2.0.3 source before touching anything: not a
#     regression from the v2.0.4 g/ɡ fix, but a real, separate,
#     pre-existing limitation of -mfa itself.
# - -mfa now auto-detects which kind of input it's looking at, and only
#     changes behavior for running text:
#     - detect_mfa_input_is_running_text() samples up to the first 500
#         non-blank lines of the input file and computes the average
#         number of whitespace-separated tokens per line. A real
#         one-word-per-line word list should average very close to 1;
#         running text (sentences, paragraphs) averages well above that.
#         Threshold: >1.5 average words/line = running text. Rewinds the
#         file handle afterward so the main read loop is unaffected.
#     - One-word-per-line input: completely unchanged. Verified
#         byte-for-byte identical to the original (aside from the
#         intentional g->ɡ difference) on a real word-list file.
#     - Running-text input: each line's ALREADY-TOKENIZED real words
#         (the same @output_words/@output_ortho arrays the rest of the
#         script already builds -- correctly handles numeral expansion,
#         letter-spelling of acronyms, etc., since each resulting piece
#         IS a legitimate word here, unlike in word-list mode where a
#         line's identity as a single headword must be preserved) are
#         added to a file-wide, deduplicated dictionary, keeping the
#         first transcription seen for any given word. Printed once,
#         sorted, after the entire file has been read -- not per line,
#         since deduplication needs the whole file first.
# - Two new flags override auto-detection when needed: --mfa-rt (force
#     running-text/dedup behavior) and --mfa-wl (force the original
#     one-word-per-line behavior). Auto-detection is only used when
#     neither is given.
# - Verified end-to-end (not just unit-level): ran real input through
#     the actual CLI (-mfa, -i, -o) for both a genuine word list (5
#     words, one per line -- output unchanged except g->ɡ) and a full
#     running-text paragraph (the one that surfaced this issue --
#     correctly produced 84 deduplicated, alphabetically sorted
#     dictionary entries, including correctly-tokenized letter-spelled
#     acronyms like "SIPM"->"ese i pe eme" and "CNT"->"se ene te", and
#     digit-by-digit numeral reading), plus both override flags forcing
#     the non-default behavior on each file.
#
