# v2.0.3
# - Changed the "g" that is outputted to the IPA "ɡ".
# - Added dental diacritics to the --mfa output metaconfig.
#
# v2.0.2
# - Also removed -mfa's trailing blank line after every entry (missed on the
#     first pass below, caught on the first real run against it): $keep_paragraphs
#     printed a separator "\n" after every input line regardless of mode,
#     which made sense back when one input line could produce a variable
#     number of output lines. Now that -mfa always emits exactly one
#     self-contained line per input line, that separator had nothing left
#     to mark. -mfa output is now a plain one-line-per-word dictionary, no
#     blank lines. Every other mode is unaffected (re-verified byte-for-byte
#     identical to v2.0.1 on real running text).
# - Fixed two related bugs in -mfa output, found while building a Chilean
#     Spanish pronunciation dictionary from a 69,427-word list: some input
#     lines silently produced the WRONG NUMBER of dictionary entries.
#   1. EXPLOSION: when line-level preprocessing turned one input headword
#       into several space-separated pseudo-words -- the ambiguous lowercase
#       Roman-letter spelling rule ("cd" -> "se de"), or institutional
#       acronym expansion ("afdd" -> "asociación de familiares de detenidos
#       desaparecidos") -- each resulting piece was written to its own MFA
#       output line, e.g. "se\ts e" / "de\td e" instead of one dictionary
#       entry "cd\ts e d e". Confirmed present for 59 headwords in one real
#       69k-word list and 682 in a real 1.1M-word list, all acronym/
#       initialism-shaped tokens and a handful of institutional acronyms.
#   2. SILENT DROP: an input line whose transcription came out completely
#       empty (e.g. a bare "ç") produced NO output line at all -- not even
#       a placeholder -- which silently merged the blank-line separator
#       before and after it into one, shifting the entry-to-input-line
#       correspondence for every subsequent line in the file by one. This
#       was found by exact line-count auditing, not by inspection -- it
#       produces no error or warning of any kind on its own.
#   Fix: -mfa now emits exactly ONE line per INPUT LINE, always, keyed on
#     the original headword captured before any line-level preprocessing
#     runs, with every buffered sub-word's phonemes joined by a single
#     space. A line with no phonemes at all still gets its headword
#     written, with an empty phoneme field, rather than disappearing.
#     Verified: re-running both real word lists above through this fix
#     produces exactly one output line per input word (zero explosions,
#     zero silent drops), byte-for-byte identical to a hand-verified
#     reference built by separately auditing and reconstructing the old
#     output. Every other output mode (default running text, -phonword,
#     -vo, -syl-list, -vrt, -coscach, -word-list) was diffed against
#     v2.0.1 on real running text and is byte-for-byte unchanged --
#     this fix only changes -mfa's own output.
#
# v2.0.1
# - Fixed line 1406 so it outputs \n instead of \r\n.
# - Fixed incorrect capture group ($4) in a single regex.
# v2.0.0 (usage-guide update)
# - The -u/--usage guide hadn't been updated to reflect everything added
#     since it was last written -- it only demonstrated F/CV/CVG/CVN/
#     manner/place output modes. Added usage examples (both languages)
#     for: -voicing (voicing output mode); --decimal-period/
#     --decimal-comma (with a worked example showing the same source
#     number read two different ways); and the meta-configurations
#     -mfa, -phonword, -vrt, -syl-list, -coscach, and -word-list, each
#     with real, verified example output generated by actually running
#     the script (not hand-written/guessed).
#
# v1.9.119
# - Simplified the Windows color fix from v1.9.118: PowerShell showed the
#     same literal escape-code clutter as legacy cmd.exe, meaning the
#     "known ANSI-capable terminal" environment-variable detection
#     (WT_SESSION/ANSICON/ConEmuANSI/TERM_PROGRAM) wasn't reliable
#     enough in practice. Replaced with a simple, unconditional rule:
#     color is disabled outright on Windows ($^O eq "MSWin32"), full
#     stop, regardless of which terminal is in use. Linux and macOS are
#     unaffected and keep full color output in every case.
#
# v1.9.118
# - FIXED: on legacy Windows cmd.exe (which doesn't process ANSI/VT100
#     escape codes by default, unlike Linux/macOS terminals and modern
#     Windows terminals like Windows Terminal/ConEmu/ANSICON), the color
#     added in earlier versions was showing up as literal escape-code
#     clutter (e.g. "←[1;32m-dp←[0m") instead of actual colors -- the
#     raw ESC byte gets displayed per the console's default OEM codepage
#     (where it renders as "←") since VT100 processing was never
#     enabled. This affected -h/-u output and the ACTIVE SETTINGS table
#     shown on every normal run.
# - FIX: added safe_colored(), a wrapper around Term::ANSIColor's
#     colored() that detects Windows and checks for a known ANSI-
#     capable terminal (WT_SESSION, ANSICON, ConEmuANSI, or
#     TERM_PROGRAM env vars); if none is found, it returns the plain,
#     uncolored text instead. All ~15 call sites that used to call
#     colored() directly (error messages, the ACTIVE SETTINGS table,
#     color_header()/color_flags() for help text) now go through this
#     wrapper. No change in behavior on Linux/macOS or modern Windows
#     terminals; legacy cmd.exe now gets clean, readable plain text
#     instead of escape-code clutter.
# - NOTE: garbled Unicode/IPA symbols in cmd.exe (e.g. "├æ" instead of
#     "Ñ") are a separate, pre-existing, and already-documented
#     limitation (cmd.exe's default codepage isn't UTF-8), unrelated to
#     this fix -- see the existing banner note about this.
#
# v1.9.117
# - FIXED the long-standing "KNOWN ISSUE" (previously printed in the
#     startup banner): cross-word resyllabification (e.g. "es el" ->
#     "e.sel", "los hombres" -> "lo.som.bres") only ever worked in
#     phonemic mode. Root cause: word_level_syllab_adjust() (the
#     function that performs the resyllabification, run as a
#     post-processing cleanup pass over the whole output file) matches
#     literal lowercase IPA phoneme characters via regex. CV/CVG/
#     CVNLRG/manner/place/voicing modes used to convert phonemes to
#     their category letters (C/V/N/L/R/G, P/N/R/T/F/L/A/X/V, etc.)
#     PER WORD, inside transcribe_word(), before that cleanup pass ever
#     ran -- so by the time word_level_syllab_adjust() saw the line, the
#     literal IPA characters it was looking for no longer existed, and
#     the cross-word merge silently never fired for these modes. Each
#     word was syllabified correctly in isolation, then simply joined
#     to its neighbors with a plain word-boundary dot.
# - FIX: moved format conversion (the CV/CVG/CVNLRG/manner/place/voicing
#     phoneme-to-letter substitution, previously in transcribe_word())
#     to run AFTER word_level_syllab_adjust() in the output-file cleanup
#     pass, instead of before. transcribe_word() now always produces
#     pure IPA phonemes regardless of output mode; format conversion
#     happens once, on the whole already-resyllabified line. Also moved
#     the upper/lowercase-adjustment step to after format conversion
#     (it used to run first, which would have corrupted the IPA
#     characters word_level_syllab_adjust needs to match, once format
#     conversion moved past it).
# - Verified against CV, CVG, CVNLRG, MANNER, PLACE, and VOICING modes:
#     syllable-boundary positions (and which segment each consonant
#     letter belongs to) now match the phonemic-mode transcription
#     exactly, syllable-for-syllable, for the same input and settings.
# - Removed the now-obsolete "KNOWN ISSUE" message from the startup
#     banner (both English and Spanish).
# - Added 5 dedicated regression test cases (resyllab_phonemic,
#     resyllab_cvnlrg, resyllab_manner, resyllab_place,
#     resyllab_voicing) covering this fix.
#
# v1.9.116
# - Fixed: a number immediately followed by a unit abbreviation with no
#     space (e.g. "50kg", "3cm", "30km/h") was being caught by the
#     mixed-alphanumeric identifier detector (Category A, in prelc_pass)
#     and spelled out letter-by-letter/digit-by-digit ("cinco cero ka ge")
#     instead of being converted to words ("cincuenta kilogramos") by the
#     dedicated unit-conversion logic. Numbers with a space before the
#     unit (e.g. "4 km²") were already working correctly; this only
#     affected the no-space form. Fixed by excluding known unit suffixes
#     from Category A identifier detection so the unit-conversion logic
#     downstream gets a chance to handle them.
# - Fixed: a standalone capitalized letter that is itself an ordinary
#     Spanish word (most commonly "Y"/"O" at the start of a sentence or
#     clause, e.g. "...es? Y una exclamación...") was being read as its
#     letter NAME ("i griega") instead of the actual word, via Category B
#     identifier detection (also in prelc_pass), which matches any
#     standalone uppercase letter outside the Roman-numeral alphabet.
#     The code's own comment already documented the intended behavior as
#     requiring length >= 2, but the regex didn't actually enforce it;
#     fixed to match the documented intent. Multi-letter identifiers
#     (NASA, IXA, etc.) are unaffected and continue to be spelled out
#     correctly.
# - Added two dedicated regression test cases (unit_no_space,
#     single_letter_conjunction) covering both fixes.
#
# v2.0.1
# - Added a new global setting, $decimal_symbol ("," or "."), controlling
#     which symbol separates whole numbers from fractions (the decimal
#     point) throughout ALL number processing (currency, percentages,
#     units/temperatures, and plain standalone decimals). The OTHER
#     symbol is always treated as the thousands (grouping) separator.
#     Settable via --decimal-period/-dp (period=decimal) or
#     --decimal-comma/-dc (comma=decimal, the default, matching the
#     existing Spanish/Chilean convention). Also settable via
#     perkins.ini (decimal_symbol = , or .).
# - This replaces several independent, ad-hoc "try both European and
#     American conventions and guess" heuristics that were previously
#     scattered across the currency/percentage/unit parsing code with a
#     single shared helper, _split_by_decimal_convention(), used
#     consistently everywhere. As a direct, intended consequence, some
#     amounts that were previously flagged as genuinely ambiguous (e.g.
#     "$1.500" -- unclear whether European 1.5 or American 1,500) are now
#     resolved unambiguously according to the explicitly declared
#     convention (e.g. "$1.500" -> "1500 pesos" under the default
#     comma-decimal convention, since "." can now only mean thousands).
# - Added the new setting to the ACTIVE SETTINGS table (NUMBERS section),
#     the perkins.ini whitelist and documentation, and -h help text (both
#     languages).
#
# v1.9.114
# - Added a version/author banner box at the very start of
#     print_console_greeting(), printed before anything else in that sub
#     (before the "file successfully read" message):
#       ===========================================
#       = Perkins v$version
#       = Scott Sadowsky · ssadowsky at g mail dot com
#       ===========================================
#     The version number is pulled from $version dynamically, not
#     hardcoded. Shown regardless of interface language.
#
# v1.9.113
# - Changed help-text section headers from bold cyan to bold blue, to be
#     more visually distinct from the bold green used for flag names.
# - color_flags() now detects "=PLACEHOLDER" style dummy values in flag
#     tokens (e.g. "--format=MODE", "--input=MyFile.txt", "-sn=SYMBOL")
#     and colors the flag part green and the placeholder part yellow,
#     making it visually obvious which part is literal and which the
#     user is meant to replace with their own value.
#
# v1.9.112
# - Added color to the -h help output: section headers are now bold cyan,
#     and flag names are bold green -- but not the commas separating
#     multiple aliases for the same flag, which stay in the default
#     color. Implemented via two new small helpers, color_header() and
#     color_flags(), built on the Term::ANSIColor colored() function
#     already used elsewhere in the script.
# - Reorganized the help text (both languages) to follow the same section
#     order as perkins.ini: MAIN OPTIONS, META-CONFIGURATIONS,
#     TRANSCRIPTION MODE OPTIONS, SPECIFIC PHONEME OPTIONS, CH SHORTCUTS
#     (now its own section, previously interleaved into SPECIFIC
