# v1.9.87
# - Fixed --syl-list/-sl always producing .PHNM.txt output instead of the
#     intended .SYLLIST.txt: the $syllable_list check in
#     assign_default_output_filename() was placed after the $output_PHON
#     check, which defaults to true, so it was unreachable in the common
#     case. Moved it earlier in the elsif chain, alongside the other
#     format-specific branches.
# - Fixed the console greeting/settings banner being silently suppressed
#     by --syl-list (and, by the same underlying mechanism, --word-list,
#     --rt/--corpus, and other meta-config presets): those presets force
#     $silent_mode = 1 internally, which in turn forces $batch_mode = 1,
#     and the greeting was gated on the live $batch_mode value. Now the
#     greeting is gated on a snapshot of $silent_mode/$batch_mode taken
#     right after defaults/perkins.ini/CLI flags are applied but BEFORE
#     any meta-config preset runs -- so the greeting only stays hidden
#     when silent/batch mode was set EXPLICITLY (by default, perkins.ini,
#     or a CLI flag), not as a preset's internal side effect.
#
# v1.9.83
# - Fixed a flood of "Use of uninitialized value" warnings (rindex,
#     pattern match, substitution, join) coming from stress_accent_routine
#     on real-world input. This sub does the same kind of legitimate
#     rindex()/@character boundary-position work as perform_syllabification
#     (already covered in v2.0.3) -- it was simply missed in that pass.
#     Added the same scoped `no warnings 'uninitialized'` treatment.
#     No logic changed; purely noise suppression for intentional,
#     pre-existing undef-as-"not found" behavior.
#
# v1.9.81
# - Added `use strict; use warnings;` to the whole script.
# - Fixed the 3 resulting "Bareword ... not allowed" compile errors by
#     quoting three string-literal defaults (pésos, eslách, becórta) that
#     were previously relying on Perl's legacy bareword-autoquoting.
# - Fixed several genuine latent bugs that `use warnings` surfaced:
#     - $eng, $esp, $word_list, $coscach_mode, $output_format, and 9
#       pause-tracking flags (sentence_break, comma_pause, etc.) were used
#       in numeric/string comparisons before ever being assigned. Gave
#       each an explicit default matching its always-intended value
#       (0 for booleans, "" for output_format). No behavioral change --
#       except that debug output (-d) now shows "0" instead of a blank
#       for these variables before they're set, which is more correct.
#     - $temp_num, used inside `eval q{...}` blocks in
#       convert_numerals_to_words() for 1-3 digit numeral conversion, was
#       NEVER declared with my/our anywhere. Under non-strict Perl this
#       silently auto-vivified as a global and worked by accident; under
#       `use strict` the eval string failed to compile, which eval
#       swallows silently -- causing s///ge to replace the matched
#       numeral with an empty string instead of its word form. This was
#       a REAL bug (silently dropped numerals under certain conditions,
#       e.g. "43 BC" support), now fixed by declaring `my $temp_num;` in
#       that subroutine. Verified via golden-master regression testing
#       that this fix restores byte-identical output to pre-strict
#       behavior.
#     - Added `no warnings 'uninitialized'` (scoped to single subs) in
#       convert_numerals_to_words, final_touches_broad_transcr, and
#       perform_syllabification, which all intentionally probe array
#       positions or an optional leading capture group that may
#       legitimately be undef/out-of-range as part of correct,
#       pre-existing boundary-detection logic -- not bugs, just noisy
#       under warnings.
# - Verified with the golden-master regression suite: after this fix,
#     output is unchanged from v2.0.2 for every case, EXCEPT the -d/--debug
#     info dump, which now prints "0" instead of a blank for a couple of
#     previously-uninitialized variables -- an intentional, expected
#     improvement, not a regression.
#
# v1.9.98
# - Fixed the "will not stay shared" closure bug in print_console_greeting's
#     nested row_str() sub: it now takes $div and $LC as explicit parameters
#     (like row3()/row2() already did) instead of closing over the outer
#     lexicals. Harmless today (print_console_greeting only runs once per
#     invocation) but was a landmine for any future refactor that called
#     it more than once. Verified with the golden-master regression suite:
#     no observable output change across all 13 test cases.
#
# v1.9.80
# - Removed dead $semi_narrow reference in the debug CLI info dump. The
#     variable was never declared or assigned anywhere in the program (a
#     leftover from a renamed/removed option); printing it always yielded
#     an uninitialized value.
# - Removed assign_lang_str and its call site. The sub was an empty no-op
#     stub, never implemented and not wired to any localization mechanism.
#     All bilingual (en/es) output is handled inline elsewhere, so no
#     functionality is lost.
#
# v1.9.75
# - Changed input file encoding from ISO-8859-1 to UTF-8.
# - Eliminated dependency on abandoned and unnecessary File::Unicode::Slurp.
# - Fixed config file (perkins.ini) processing, It not allows you to set every
#   possible variable in the program.
# - Created a perkins.ini skeleton file with all variables listed but commented
#     out so users can take advantage of it far more easily.
# - Modified pause processing so "!" and "?" are handled the same way as "." is
#     (as a long pause, assuming user wants to retain pauses).
# - Fixed collision of the "-sl" flag, which was assigned to both slash processing
#     and single line processing. The long forms of these options always worked
#     properly.
# - Fixed edge case of handling of negative sign at left edge of numbers. It was
#     interpreted correctly EXCEPT when followed by a comma and space (e.g. in
#     "Allá, -30 grados es común" the "-" was dropped due to incorrect processing
#     of pauses earlier in the script.
# - Fixed handling of dollar/peso sign at beginning of positive and negative numbers.
#     previously it was left in place (e.g. "pesos mil doscientos"). Now, it is moved
#     to the end of the amount (e.g. "mil doscientos pesos"), which is how it's said.
# - Suppressed program banner except when help (-h) and usage (-u) are called.
# - Added routine to show all ACTIVE variables and their values whenever the program is run.
# - Fixed incorrect handling of manner of articulation: vowels were formerly converted
#     into dashes; now they are properly displayed as "V".
# - Added functionality (triggered by -mfa) to produce MFA-compatible output for segment-
#     level transcriptions. This consists of one item per line output made up of the
#     orthographic word (in lower case and with tildes removed), a tab, and the phonemic
#     transcription of the word with a space between each phoneme (with no stress marking,
#     no syllable division, and using the same phoneme inventory as the target MFA acoustic
#     model).
# - Added functionality (triggered by -phonword or -pw) that produces a different output,
#     for word-level transcription, geared toward Praat textgrids and other types of
#     linguistic work. This function produces on each line the orthographic word with
#     capitalization and tildes maintained, a tab, and a phonemic word transcription that
#     maintains syllable divisions and stress marking. This is geared toward Praat word-level
#     segments. Note that this produces more accurate phonemic representations than -MFA,
#     which adheres to the MFA usage (doubtlessly motivated by ASCII compatibility) of using
#     /t/ for /t̪/, /d/ for /d̪/, /tʃ/ for /t͡ʃ/ (or the single character variant).
# - Fixed an inconsistency when processing "ió" to maintain hiatus (e.g. "crió" /kɾi.ˈo/,
#     "guión" /gi.ˈon/) when appropriate, but to produce a diphthong when relevant (e.g.
#     situación /si.t̪wa.ˈsjon/).
# - Fixed issues when processing various allomorphs of -uir verbs.
# - Fixed a series of additional diphthong processing edge-case bugs.
# - Improved processing of numbers.
# - Greatly improved handling of Roman numerals. They MUST be capitalized to be recognized
#     as such. Otherwise, they're processed as lexical items if appropriate ("vi" from "ver") or
#     not processed at all.
# - Added handling of times (e.g. 1:30, 21:28).
# - Added full support for mathematical operations.
# - Fixed heretofore basic handling of negative numbers.
# - Added support for multiple currencies.
# - Made the currency unit represented by "$" configurable ($moneda = ...). Must be given as plural.
# - Normalized decimal processing in large numbers.
# - Greatly improved handling of currencies.
# - Improved handling of odd punctuation (e.g. "..", ",,")
# - Fixed handling of dates so they're never misinterpreted as mathematical expressions.
# - Specifically excluded US-style dates (e.g. 12/31/2000). They just cause problems are are very
#   unlikely to be found in the wild.
# - Added additional date formats (e.g. ISO-8601).
# - Added support for temperatures.
#   · All positive and negative numbers followed by °C, °F, ºC, ºF, oC, oF are treated as temperatures.
# - Added support for ordinal numbers.
#   · Numerals from 1-10 are processed according to their idiosyncrasies.
#   · Positive numerals up to 100 that have º, ª, ° attached are converted to ordinals (e.g. 4º = cuarto).
#   · All positive numerals followed by o, a, ero, era, eros, eras, vo, va, etc. are converted to ordinals.
# - Other minor bug fixes.
# - Drastically cleaned up code base.

# TODO
# - Document -phonword in help and elsewhere.
# - Document -mfa option in help and elsewhere.
# - Add missing flags (from here) to config file.
# - Reorganize configuration definitions here and then in the help file to be more logical.
# - Add options to set specific "ch" conversions (direct to single char, multichar with
#   retraction and tiebar, multicar with just tiebar, multichar with just retraction, and
#   multichar with nothing. "ch" as single char seems to already exist.
# - Advise user that on linux and mac, chmod +x perkins.bin is likely necessary,
#   depending on how I package it.


#################################################################################
#                 SET DEFAULT VALUES FOR PROGRAM OPTIONS AND VARIABLES          #
#################################################################################

###################################################
#         OPTIONS THAT USERS MAY CONFIGURE        #
# These options can be edited here, or overridden #
#   in perkins.ini without changing the script    #
###################################################

# USER OPTIONS - PROGRAM BEHAVIOR
our $debug               = 0;
our $debug_syllab_sub    = 0;
our $debug_to_logfile    = 0;
our $silent_mode         = 0;
our $batch_mode          = 0;    # Set to 1 for batch mode. This suppresses output to stdout, etc.
my $use_config_file      = 0;    # Read perkins.ini if present.
my $config_filename      = "perkins.ini"; # Name of the configuration file to read.
                                          # Change with --config-file/-cf on the command line.
my $lang                 = "en"; # Default program message language

# META OPTIONS - FOR SPECIFIC COMBINATIONS OF VARIABLES
our $corpus_running_text = 0;    # Select settings for the Coscach / Codicach: Running text syllabified at sentence
                                 #   level, one character per phoneme, etc. This overrides most other settings, to make
                                 #   sure that the corpus is processed uniformly, so turn it off to specify other options.

our $syllable_list       = 0;    # Select settings for producing output that is easy to process at the syllable level (each
                                 #   syllable is separated from others by a space). This overrides most other settings, to make
                                 #   sure that the corpus is processed uniformly, so turn it off to specify other options.

our $mfa_format          = 0;
our $protect_markers     = 1;    # [..] is a transcription marker,
                                 # not speech. --no-markers to disable.
our $marker_symbol       = '???';  # what a marker becomes. --marker-symbol=X
our $token_mode          = 0;    # --tokens / -tok: TAB-separated tokens in, one TAB-separated
                                 # transcription per token out, narrow rules applied across the
                                 # whole line.

################################################################################
# BUILT-IN PRONUNCIATION TABLES                                                #
# Every special reading Perkins knows, applied to whole tokens before any      #
# other processing. Built into the program, not read from a file, so they are  #
# always available.                                                            #
#                                                                              #
# %BUILTIN_RESPELL: token (EXACT case) -> ordinary Spanish spelling that       #
#   Perkins then transcribes in every output mode. A written accent marks the  #
#   stressed letter; a space keeps two letters out of a diphthong. An empty    #
#   respelling removes the token. Mostly acronyms and codes read as letters,   #
#   and words whose reading differs from the default.                          #
# %BUILTIN_IPA: token (exact case, then lowercase) -> a phonemic               #
#   transcription used as is: backchannels and fillers, which cannot be        #
#   respelled. mm is deliberately absent: whether a given mm is a filler or    #
#   millimetres is a judgement call, so it keeps Perkins' ordinary handling.   #
# %BUILTIN_RESPELL_ANYCASE: token (matched in lowercase) -> respelling, as     #
#   %BUILTIN_RESPELL, looked up after the exact-case table and %BUILTIN_IPA,   #
#   so an exact-case entry (AND, UP, WoW, RR) keeps its own reading. Letter    #
#   names are joined, with the stress on the last letter (psu peeseú).         #
# A key ending in a period (J., Dr., C.C.U.) matches only with the period.     #
# Sources: the COSCACH inventory, reviewed 261030 (exact case);                #
#   the respellings of the COSCACH spelling review (any case).                 #
################################################################################
